<h2>Demo 1 — the emergency trade</h2>
<p>Built the way a plumber's site has to be built: for someone standing in water at 11pm who will call whoever they trust first. Tappable phone number inside the first second, PGDB registration where people actually look, emergency and after-hours terms stated plainly instead of buried.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>What to look at:</strong> how fast the number is reachable on a phone, and what the site tells a panicking visitor in the first five seconds.</li>
  <li>The service pages are written around symptoms people actually search — blocked drain, no hot water — not trade jargon.</li>
  <li>Trust signals (registration, insurance, guarantees) sit in the header and above the fold, not the footer.</li>
</ul>
<p><a href="/demos/redwrench-plumbing/" class="btn large">VIEW THE DEMO — REDWRENCH PLUMBING →</a></p>

<h2>Demo 2 — the considered trade</h2>
<p>The opposite job: a builder's customer researches for weeks, asks three mates, and checks whether you're the sort of outfit that still exists in five years. This build is structured for that — LBP status explained, a project gallery that shows process rather than just glamour shots, and copy that answers the questions people are embarrassed to ask.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>What to look at:</strong> how the site earns trust across a longer visit — credentials, process, and proof laid out in the order a cautious customer actually wants them.</li>
  <li>The enquiry form asks builder-specific questions, because a renovation enquiry and a drainlaying enquiry are different conversations.</li>
  <li>Every page still works on a phone with two bars, because that's where half the research happens.</li>
</ul>
<p><a href="/demos/framewright-builders/" class="btn large">VIEW THE DEMO — FRAMEWRIGHT BUILDERS →</a></p>

